  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe mid level disturbance will exit north into Colorado early \nThursday and leave behind a worked-over atmosphere that will take \nsome time to destabilize with daytime heating. At the same time, \nPWATs will trend down Thursday across western NM as the Monsoon \nmoisture plume leans east. PoPs have trended down Thursday as a \nresult and a more limited round of convection is now expected. \nThat said, grounds will be saturated, so less rainfall will be \nneeded to cause flash flooding Thursday. What remains of the \nPacific low/trough will get caught up in the westerlies and move \nacross NM as a shortwave trough on Friday, interacting with left- \nover moisture and producing a round of showers and storms with \ncontinued flash flood potential, especially from Ruidoso east to \nthe TX border. The PWAT downtrend will continue into Saturday, \nwith the westerlies bringing some warming and drying through the\nweekend. A Pacific trough/low is forecast to move through the\nregion early next week, potentially interacting with moisture from\nmajor Hurricane Polo and bringing another round of showers and\nstorms with soaking rainfall to much of NM.",
